{% comment %}
  Appx — Product Specs Table. Resolves the current product to its assigned template HANDLE from the
  shop routing map + this product's routing shard (features 43 + 108).

  Params (render scope is isolated — all passed explicitly):
  - product: the current product.
  - routing: the parsed `shop.metafields["$app"].routing.value`, in the COMPACT delivery shape from
             `compactRoutingForDelivery`. WIRE v3 — BROAD TIERS ONLY:
               { v, handles[], def, byType, byVendor, byCollection }
             Every by*/def value is an INDEX into `handles`. A private wire contract with that
             function; the two move together.
  - shard:   THIS product's `$app:appx_routing_shard` metaobject (feature 108), passed in by the
             block. Two json fields, read as `.value`:
               by_product : { "<product id>": "<template handle STRING>" }  (D3)
               excluded   : { "<product id>": 1 }                           (membership)
             A product in a sparse bucket gets `shard == nil`, so both lookups are nil — a clean miss
             that falls through to the broad tiers.

  Emits the resolved handle STRING, or nothing (the block CAPTURES this output; a snippet can't return).

  Resolution order (data-model §9, feature 45 Decision B). The ACTIVE set is disjoint, so at most one
  key matches — the order is efficiency, not precedence. An EXPLICIT single-product assignment
  (`shard.by_product`) is checked BEFORE the exclude gate, so an excluded product still reaches its own
  dedicated table; the exclude gate then carves products out only of the BROAD tiers:
    shard.by_product[<product id>]
      -> shard.excluded? -> nothing
      -> byType[type] -> byVendor[vendor]
        -> byCollection[<collection id>] (scan) -> def
  The per-product tier resolves to a handle STRING directly (D3); each broad tier resolves to a handle
  INDEX (`hidx`) dereferenced through `routing.handles` once, only if the exclude gate let us in.

  SHAPE NOTES:
  - Keys are the BARE numeric id, so build `'' | append: product.id` (a string key to match the JSON
    keys), not a `gid://` token. Same idiom builds the collection key.
  - A broad-tier lookup returns a handle INDEX. The test is `!= blank`, never truthiness: index 0 is
    valid and `0 == blank` is false, but an absent key is nil (blank). Dereferencing `handles` once
    guards against `handles[nil]` coercing nil -> 0 and returning the wrong table. The per-product tier
    needs no such guard — its value is already the final handle string (or nil).
  - byType / byVendor keys are raw selector strings (a blank type/vendor indexes a nonexistent key).
    TAG routing is NOT read — the compact shape omits `byTag` (post-MVP; always empty).

  Collections are scanned in 50-item chunks (Shopify caps `for` at 50), breaking on the first hit.
{% endcomment %}
{%- liquid
  assign hidx = ''
  assign handle = ''

  if routing != blank
    assign pid = '' | append: product.id

    # Explicit single-product assignment wins even for an excluded product (Decision B), checked
    # BEFORE the exclude gate. From this product's shard; the value is the final handle STRING (D3).
    assign handle = shard.by_product.value[pid]

    # The broad tiers are carved out by the shard's `excluded` set: an excluded product matches none.
    if handle == blank
      unless shard.excluded.value[pid] != blank
        assign hidx = routing.byType[product.type]

        if hidx == blank
          assign hidx = routing.byVendor[product.vendor]
        endif

        if hidx == blank
          assign coll_chunks = product.collections.size | plus: 49 | divided_by: 50
          for chunk in (1..coll_chunks)
            assign coll_offset = chunk | minus: 1 | times: 50
            for collection in product.collections offset: coll_offset limit: 50
              assign cid = '' | append: collection.id
              assign coll_hit = routing.byCollection[cid]
              if coll_hit != blank
                assign hidx = coll_hit
                break
              endif
            endfor
            if hidx != blank
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif

        if hidx == blank
          assign hidx = routing.def
        endif

        # Dereference the broad-tier handle table ONCE, guarded so a miss (nil hidx) never indexes
        # handles[0] by nil-coercion.
        if hidx != blank
          assign handle = routing.handles[hidx]
        endif
      endunless
    endif
  endif

  echo handle
-%}
